---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GifAudioToMp3 from '@/components/tools/media/GifAudioToMp3.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="wav-to-mp3">
  <GifAudioToMp3
    client:load
    accept=".wav,.wave,.aiff,.aif,.aifc,.bwf,audio/wav,audio/wave,audio/aiff"
    sourceLabel="WAV"
    sourceHint="Session bounces, field recordings and interviews, including files of several hundred megabytes"
    target="mp3"
    defaultBitrate="192"
    emphasiseRatio={true}
    formatNote="WAV is uncompressed, so this is the conversion with the largest possible saving. The result panel shows exactly what percentage of the original each file ends up at."
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Large WAV files convert locally</h2>
    <p>
      WAV files are large. A one-hour stereo interview is about 600 MB; a multitrack bounce or a field
      recording can be several gigabytes. A converter that uploads the file must receive, store, encode and
      return all of it, so upload services often limit file size or files per hour.
    </p>
    <p>
      This WAV to MP3 converter runs the LAME encoder in a WebAssembly build of FFmpeg inside this browser tab,
      so the file stays on your computer and there is no queue. The limit is your own memory: the browser
      sandbox has about 2 GB of working memory, enough for a three-hour stereo recording.
    </p>

    <h2>How much smaller the MP3 is</h2>
    <p>
      Standard CD-rate stereo WAV is 1411 kbps. The MP3 size is your chosen bitrate divided by that number.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>MP3 setting</th><th>Share of the WAV</th><th>One hour, stereo</th><th>One hour, mono</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>320 kbps</td><td>22.7%</td><td>≈ 137 MB</td><td>≈ 137 MB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>256 kbps</td><td>18.1%</td><td>≈ 110 MB</td><td>≈ 110 MB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>192 kbps</td><td>13.6%</td><td>≈ 82 MB</td><td>≈ 82 MB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>128 kbps</td><td>9.1%</td><td>≈ 55 MB</td><td>≈ 55 MB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>96 kbps</td><td>6.8%</td><td>≈ 41 MB</td><td>≈ 41 MB</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      MP3 bitrate is the total for the file, so mono alone does not halve the size. But the whole bitrate goes to
      one channel, so a mono voice recording at 96 kbps sounds like a stereo one at 192 kbps. For a
      single-voice interview, mono at 96 kbps gives the largest saving.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing a bitrate by content</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Single-voice interview or dictation: 96 kbps mono. It sounds the same as the WAV on headphones and is about 40 MB an hour.</li>
      <li>Podcast with two or three speakers: 128 kbps, the platform standard. A higher bitrate is inaudible on a commute.</li>
      <li>Live recording, room ambience, applause: 192 kbps stereo. Low bitrates handle diffuse noise and audience sound worst.</li>
      <li>Music mixdown for another person: 256 or 320 kbps. Also send the WAV if they will master it.</li>
      <li>Audio for more editing: do not convert. Keep the WAV; MP3 is a delivery format, not a working format.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When to switch on loudness normalisation</h2>
    <p>
      The advanced panel has an EBU R128 loudness normaliser with a target of −16 LUFS and a −1.5 dBTP ceiling,
      the level that podcast platforms and most speech distributors expect.
    </p>
    <p>
      Turn it on for interviews, lectures, meeting recordings and recordings from several sessions at different
      gain settings, so listeners do not have to keep changing the volume. Leave it off for music that is
      already mastered, because it overrides the mastering, and for audio you will master yourself later.
    </p>

    <h2>AIFF, BWF and other uncompressed relatives</h2>
    <p>
      This page also accepts AIFF and AIFC (the Apple equivalent of WAV, common from Logic and older Mac tools)
      and Broadcast Wave files. BWF is a normal WAV with an extra <code>bext</code> chunk for timecode,
      originator and coding history. The audio decodes the same, but MP3 has no equivalent for the broadcast
      metadata, so it is dropped. If you need that metadata, keep the BWF with the MP3.
    </p>

    <h2>A note on 32-bit float sources</h2>
    <p>
      Recorders such as the Zoom F-series and MixPre write 32-bit float WAV, where samples can go above 0 dBFS
      without clipping. These files convert here, but anything above full scale is limited in the MP3. If your
      recording has very loud peaks, lower the gain in an editor first, so the encoder does not decide where
      to clip.
    </p>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      To convert the other way for editing, use <a href="/mp3-to-wav">MP3 to WAV</a>. For a lossless archive at
      about half the size of WAV, convert to FLAC in the <a href="/audio-converter">general audio
      converter</a>. To join several takes with trims and crossfades before encoding, use the
      <a href="/audio-joiner">audio joiner</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
